One source text per script, shared by its functions and by contexts.

The parser copied each function's text when the function's parse ended
(js_strndup) and kept the copies until the whole script was parsed and
js_create_function shared them (patch 0006). The text of a function
nested n levels deep was therefore held n times while a script compiled:
an 860 KB bundle wrapped in one function held 2.2 MB of such copies at
its peak. Every context that compiled the same script also kept its own
copy of the text.

The parser now only notes where a function's text lies in its buffer.
When the script is parsed, js_fd_assign_sources gives each function its
text: when the outermost functions make up half of the script or more,
one JSSourceText holds the whole script and every function points into
it; otherwise each outermost function gets a text and its nested
functions share it, as before. A whole-script text of 1 KB or more is
found again by its content (length, hash, bytes) through
JSRuntime.source_text_list, so the documents of one page that load the
same script share one text. Function.prototype.toString returns the same
characters as before.

JS_ComputeMemoryUsage counts each text once (js_func_source_count,
js_func_source_size, included in js_func_size).

Measured on the host with an 860 KB minified script: the peak of one
compile fell from 11.6 MB to 9.4 MB, and three documents with that
script settle at 16.4 MB instead of 18.2 MB.

